\nAlternatively, discard J1, which is a single honor tile. \n\nWhich is better? \n\nIf I discard J1, then my pairs are B5, F2, J3. The remaining tiles are W1, W2, T4, T9, B6, B7, F3. \n\nStill, I need to form more pairs. \n\nIf I discard T4, then my pairs are B5, F2, J3. Remaining tiles: W1, W2, T9, B6, B7, F3, J1. \n\nStill need to form more pairs. \n\nBut maybe discarding J1 is better because it's an honor tile, which is less useful. \n\nAlternatively, discard F3, which is a single. \n\nHmm. \n\nAnother angle: the current hand has a pair of B5s. If I can form a Pure Straight, which requires three chows in the same suit. Let's see: B5, B6, B7 is one chow. If I can form two more. For example, B3, B4, B5 and B6, B7, B8. But I don't have those. \n\nAlternatively, maybe a Pure Shifted Chows. For example, B4, B5, B6 and B5, B6, B7. But I don't have B4. \n\nNot helpful. \n\nAlternatively, maybe a Pure Terminal Chows. But I don't have 1,2,3 or 7,8,9 in the same suit. \n\nSo perhaps the best bet is to discard a tile that is not part of any possible chow or pung. \n\nLooking at the options, J1 is a single honor tile. Discarding it would be safe. \n\nSo the answer would be Play J1.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Current hand has potential for Seven Pairs with three existing pairs (B5, F2, J3), but needs to discard non-paired tiles to form additional pairs. Discarding J1 (a single honor tile) preserves other pair-building opportunities.]\nReason: [Discarding J1 removes an unnecessary single honor tile, maintaining the possibility to form more pairs with remaining tiles (W1, W2, T4, T9, B6, B7, F3) while keeping concealed status for +4 points.]\nAnswer: Play J1"}
