air. \n\nBut to form the B7-B8-B9 Chow, I need to have those three tiles. Since I have all three in hand, I can form that. So if I discard, say, B7, but wait, I can't discard because I need to keep them for the Chow. Wait, no. To form a Chi, I need to have two tiles in hand and take one from the discard. But in this case, I already have all three in hand. So maybe I can form a concealed Chi? Wait, no. In Mahjong, a Chi is formed by taking a discarded tile. If you have two in hand and take the third from a discard, you can form a Chi. But if you have all three in hand, you can't form a Chi unless you have already discarded one. Wait, but in this case, the B7, B8, B9 are all in hand. So maybe I can form a concealed Chow? No, Chows can't be concealed; they must be formed by taking a discarded tile. Wait, no. Wait, in Chinese Mahjong, a concealed set is a Pung or Kong, not a Chow. Chows must be formed by taking a discarded tile. So if I have all three in hand, I can't form a Chi unless I discard one and then take another. Wait, but if I have all three, I can't form a Chi unless I have already taken one from a discard. Hmm, this is confusing. \n\nWait, maybe I'm misunderstanding. If I have all three B7, B8, B9 in my hand, I can't form a Chi because that requires taking a tile from the discard. So I can't form a Chi for B7-B8-B9 unless I have two in hand and take the third from a discard. But in this case, I have all three. So maybe I can't form a Chi for that. Therefore, those three B tiles would have to be a sequence in the hand, but since they are all in hand, they can't be a Chi. So maybe they are just three individual tiles. Then how can I form a fourth meld?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the B7-B8-B9 can be part of a different pattern. For example, if I can form a pair with F3 and J3, but that's two different tiles. So that's not possible. Wait, I have one F3 and one J3. So that's two different tiles. So I can't make a pair from them. Therefore, I need to have a pair. May