ds are part of the hand, but when you form a meld, you take tiles from your hand and the discard. \n\nThis is getting a bit confusing. Let me try to reconstruct. \n\nThe melds are [[W2, W3, W4], [W1, W1, W1]]. So to form the first Chi, you had two W2 and one W4 in your hand, and took the W3 from the previous player. Then the Peng of W1 requires you to have two W1 in your hand and take the third from the discard. \n\nSo the initial hand had: \n\n- For the Chi [W2, W3, W4]: you had two W2 and one W4, then took W3. So after forming the Chi, you have one W2 and one W4 left. \n\n- For the Peng [W1, W1, W1]: you had two W1 and took one from the discard. So after Peng, you have zero W1 left. \n\nThen the remaining tiles in your hand are: \n\nOriginal 13 tiles minus the ones used in melds. Let's see:\n\nOriginal 13 tiles:\n\n- W2 (2), W3 (1), W4 (1) \u2192 used in Chi, so after Chi: W2 (1), W4 (1) left.\n\n- W1 (2) \u2192 used in Peng, so after Peng: 0 left.\n\nPlus the other tiles: B3, B7, B8, B9, J3, and W5. \n\nSo total tiles in hand after melds: \n\nW2 (1), W4 (1), W5 (1), B3 (1), B7 (1), B8 (1), B9 (1), J3 (1) \u2192 8 tiles. That's correct. \n\nNow, with 8 tiles, I need to form two more melds and a pair. \n\nPossible options:\n\n1. B7, B8, B9 as a Chi. Then remaining tiles: W2, W4, W5, J3. \n\nHow to form two more melds and a pair from these four tiles? \n\nIf J3 is the pair, then the remaining three tiles (W2, W4, W5) need to form a meld. But they can't form a Chi (needs consecutive numbers) or a Pung (needs three of the same). So this is a problem. \n\nAlternative approach: Maybe there's a different way to form the melds. \n\nWhat if I don't form the B7, B8, B9 as a Chi? Maybe use B3 in a different way. \n\nB3 is part of the melds of the previous player: they have a Chi [B2, B3, B4]. So if I discard B3, the previous player might have already used it, so it's less likely they need it. \n\nAlternatively, if I keep B3 and form a Pung with it. But I only have one B3. \n